WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.826
Lesson 3 teaches kinetic energy, but the real intellectual payoff is proportional reasoning.

2
00:00:04.826 --> 00:00:09.652
Motion Store depends on both load and speed, yet those two factors do not behave symmetrically.

3
00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:14.478
Load enters directly.

4
00:00:14.478 --> 00:00:19.305
Speed enters through a square.

5
00:00:19.305 --> 00:00:24.131
That means doubling mass doubles the store, while doubling speed quadruples it.

6
00:00:24.131 --> 00:00:28.957
Students often remember the formula but miss the pattern.

7
00:00:28.957 --> 00:00:33.783
This video is designed to prevent that.

8
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:38.609
The worked example gets much easier when the learner already knows which variable has the stronger effect before any numbers are substituted.

9
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:43.435
Keep this takeaway in view: Motion Store becomes intuitive when students stop treating speed like an ordinary one-step factor.

10
00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:48.262
Two factors means mass and speed.

11
00:00:48.262 --> 00:00:53.088
Both matter, but they do not affect Motion Store in the same way.

12
00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:57.914
Big surprise means speed is squared.

13
00:00:57.914 --> 00:01:02.740
That is why changes in speed can dominate the comparison.

14
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:09.902
This diagram matters because it turns a hidden algebra feature into a visible pattern.

15
00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:17.065
One comparison keeps the same mass and raises the speed.

16
00:01:17.065 --> 00:01:24.227
Another keeps the same speed and raises the mass.

17
00:01:24.227 --> 00:01:31.390
The learner can see that the motion-energy jump is much sharper in the speed comparison.

18
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:38.553
The board helps students explain not just what the answer is, but why the answer is changing so strongly.

19
00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:45.715
Keep this takeaway in view: A clear comparison board makes the speed-squared effect something students can reason with, not just memorize.

20
00:01:45.715 --> 00:01:52.878
Mass comparison: Changing mass matters, but it scales the store directly.

21
00:01:52.878 --> 00:02:00.040
Speed comparison: Changing speed can produce a much larger jump because the formula squares it.

22
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.242
Imagine a sound system with one control that raises output steadily and another that ramps the output much more aggressively.

23
00:02:05.242 --> 00:02:10.443
Mass behaves like the steady control.

24
00:02:10.443 --> 00:02:15.645
Speed behaves like the more dramatic control.

25
00:02:15.645 --> 00:02:20.847
The analogy is not perfect physics, but it helps students feel the asymmetry.

26
00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:26.048
The goal is to stop saying speed and mass both matter in the same way.

27
00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:31.250
They do not.

28
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:36.452
The squared-speed effect gives speed a very different role in the energy story.

29
00:02:36.452 --> 00:02:41.653
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner feel that mass and speed are both important but not equally influential in Motion Store.

30
00:02:41.653 --> 00:02:46.855
Steady control means mass.

31
00:02:46.855 --> 00:02:52.057
When mass changes, the Motion Store changes in a direct, linear way.

32
00:02:52.057 --> 00:02:57.258
Aggressive control means speed.

33
00:02:57.258 --> 00:03:02.460
Speed has a stronger influence because the formula uses speed squared.

34
00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.693
The formal statement is kinetic energy equals one half m v squared.

35
00:03:07.693 --> 00:03:12.927
The one-half and the mass matter, but the most important conceptual feature is the v squared term.

36
00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:18.160
That is the part students need to notice early.

37
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.393
If speed doubles, v squared becomes four times larger.

38
00:03:23.393 --> 00:03:28.627
If speed triples, v squared becomes nine times larger.

39
00:03:28.627 --> 00:03:33.860
Those comparison facts are what give the worked example its shape.

40
00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:39.093
Keep this takeaway in view: The kinetic-energy formula becomes much more powerful when students read its scaling behavior before doing arithmetic.

41
00:03:39.093 --> 00:03:44.327
Direct scaling: Doubling mass doubles Motion Store if speed stays fixed.

42
00:03:44.327 --> 00:03:49.560
Squared scaling: Doubling speed quadruples Motion Store if mass stays fixed.

43
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.793
kinetic energy = 1/2 mv^2.

44
00:03:54.793 --> 00:04:00.027
mass changes the store directly.

45
00:04:00.027 --> 00:04:05.260
Read the v squared term as a reasoning clue before using the equation numerically.

46
00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:11.296
A strong routine for Lesson 3 is to compare the factor changes before computing the exact value.

47
00:04:11.296 --> 00:04:17.332
Ask first whether mass changed, speed changed, or both.

48
00:04:17.332 --> 00:04:23.368
If speed changed, ask whether the square will dominate the comparison.

49
00:04:23.368 --> 00:04:29.404
Only then move into the full calculation.

50
00:04:29.404 --> 00:04:35.440
This routine matters because it teaches students to see structure in the formula instead of using it as a black box.

51
00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.476
Keep this takeaway in view: The comparison-first routine keeps the speed-squared insight alive all the way into the final answer.

52
00:04:41.476 --> 00:04:47.512
Step 1 means find the changed factor.

53
00:04:47.512 --> 00:04:53.548
Work out whether the comparison is about mass, speed, or both.

54
00:04:53.548 --> 00:04:59.584
Step 2 means notice the square.

55
00:04:59.584 --> 00:05:05.620
If speed changed, the v squared effect should become part of the reasoning immediately.

56
00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.990
Try four checks.

57
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.360
Does Motion Store depend on mass and speed?

58
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.730
Yes.

59
00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.100
If mass doubles with speed fixed, what happens?

60
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.470
The store doubles.

61
00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.840
If speed doubles with mass fixed, what happens?

62
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.210
The store quadruples.

63
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.580
And why does speed have the stronger effect?

64
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.950
Because it is squared in the formula.

65
00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.320
Those are the key reasoning moves behind the lesson.

66
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.690
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: Motion Store can now be read as a scaling story instead of a memorized expression.

67
00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:46.060
Mass rule means direct.

68
00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:49.430
With speed fixed, doubling mass doubles the Motion Store.

69
00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.800
Speed rule means squared.

70
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.170
With mass fixed, doubling speed quadruples the Motion Store.

71
00:05:56.170 --> 00:06:02.351
You are ready for the worked example now.

72
00:06:02.351 --> 00:06:08.532
Begin by deciding whether the story changes mass, speed, or both.

73
00:06:08.532 --> 00:06:14.713
If speed changes, make the square part of your reasoning before any arithmetic starts.

74
00:06:14.713 --> 00:06:20.894
Then write the one-half m v squared formula and substitute carefully.

75
00:06:20.894 --> 00:06:27.075
The best M3 Lesson 3 answers show that the student saw the strong speed effect coming before the calculation confirmed it.

76
00:06:27.075 --> 00:06:33.255
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: Motion Store now has a clear scaling logic as well as a usable equation.

77
00:06:33.255 --> 00:06:39.436
Step 1: Read the comparison before you reach for substitution.

78
00:06:39.436 --> 00:06:45.617
Step 2: Keep the v squared feature explicit in your reasoning.

79
00:06:45.617 --> 00:06:51.798
identify whether mass, speed, or both are changing.

80
00:06:51.798 --> 00:06:57.979
predict the comparison effect, especially the speed-squared part.

81
00:06:57.979 --> 00:07:04.160
The worked example rewards students who can see the structure of the formula before they compute the number.
